“Hermione, could you ever just look mediocre for once?” Daphne teased, brushing a hand through her curls.

“This dress is amazing,” Astoria gushed, looking over at Theo with a frown. “Why do you never dress me with diamonds?” She winged.

The girls were both dressed in silk, Astoria in sunset orange and Daphne in cobalt blue. “Because I don’t have money growing trees like this tosser does, Story.”
Draco snorted. “Alright that’s enough of all the fucking fashion admiring. I need a drink.”

He pulled on her arm but she stayed put, rooted to the ground in her heels. His eyes flashed for a split second with a threat until he reeled it back in.

“I’ll stay here,” she said firmly.

She watched the thick muscles in his jaw roll under the surface of his pale skin as he digested her words.

“Stay here,” he warned.

Hermione grit her teeth with the same feeling of lost dependency that she had dealt with for years. “I’m not a child, Draco. I’m quite capable of being without you.”
He raised a sharp brow. “Yeah, I bet you fucking are.” Rolling her eyes she shoved at his shoulder. “Just go.”
Draco’s face was a tight mixture of amusement and restraint as he slammed his lips onto hers before very reluctantly letting go and heading to the bar. It didn’t take long before he was back by her side, pushing a flute of fizzy champagne into her hand, a tumbler filled with ice and firewhisky in his own. They stood in their little circle of Slytherin friends and drank, Draco held her, his hands never leaving her body for even a second, they only tightened with the lingering eyes around the room.

His body was a coiled spring begging to be let loose and the more he drank the more she could feel the heat of hostility burning out from his body and into hers, putting her on edge. She felt him dip his head low against her cheek, his hot breath ghosting across her lips.

“Let’s dance,” he slurred, it was a testament to how drunk he was for him to be suggesting they dance instead of her.

He yanked her onto the dance floor, giving her only a second to drop her glass on a nearby table before it smashed across the floor.

He wrapped his arms around her waist, his forehead resting against hers. It had always been his preferred way of connecting to her without sex. It would have been comforting had it not been for his silver eyes drilling into hers with their unnecessary intensity. He spun her and dipped her with impressive grace across the dance floor, weaving in between other dancing couples. She could see Blaise and Theo with their girls doing the same and it brought a smile to her face to see them all so genuinely happy.

He drenched her neck with gentle kisses while droplets of gold bounced across the marble from her dress as he got handsy. “I’m going to wreck you tonight,” he whispered, slipping his velvet tongue between her lips.

Hermione stiffened with his words. You would think after ten years of being subjected to his twisted ways she would have built up a tolerance but she never did. He always used silky threats wrapped in kisses, it was nothing new but it was something that never got easier either.

Theo appeared at her side, smiley and innocent as he asked for a dance. “You can absolutely get fucked,” Draco snapped.
Theo’s face screwed up in irritation. “Don’t be like that mate, I just want to steal her for one dance, I’ll bring her straight back,” he winked, trying to lighten Draco's sudden dark mood.

Draco pulled her behind him as he stepped forward, inches from Theo, their chests almost touching as he gritted out his refusal to let her dance with anyone that wasn’t him into his face.

“Get.. fucked.”

“Draco, stop it!” Hermione hissed, trying to pull his concrete body back a few steps.

Hermione only caught a glimpse of Theo’s sad expression as Draco pulled her like a doll up the stairs and out onto the balcony, shoving a lit cigarette into her hand and lighting his own.

Hermione wasted no time in stubbornly flicking the unsmoked cigarette over the edge of the railings to die out on the cold grounds below. Draco huffed an amused laugh, thick smoke billowing out from his nostrils across her face.

“Well that was stupid,” he observed flatly.

“What the fuck is wrong with you? Why do you have such a thing against Theo? I thought he was your friend?” She asked, crossing her arms over her bejewelled chest.

He raised a brow, holding his cigarette between his thumb and pointer finger and slipped it between his lips slowly, Hermione watched the orange tip glow brighter before he spoke.

“Nott is my friend when he’s not actively trying to steal my wife from me.”

She threw her hands up in disbelief. “You’ve got to be joking! Draco, he has a girlfriend. No one is trying to steal me, that’s your own insecurities talking.”

She took quick steps backwards as he walked towards her, a mad glint in his irises. Her lower back hit the silver railings, digging into her skin the more she was forced to arch her back over them. He was glued to the front of her body, his face a breath away from hers, looking frantically between her eyes with an expression that could freeze fire. Possession fuelling every action he made against her and anyone else.

“Girlfriend or not, he wants you. I’ve seen the way he looks at you, the way they all fucking look at you and I can’t stand it. You’re mine Hermione, I’ve said it once and I will say it again—that is all you will ever be.”

Her chin quivered with the harsh finality of his little speech, her mouth opened to reply and was very quickly silenced with his, he never wanted to listen to what she had to say because why would it matter?

Hermione couldn’t understand how a heart could continue to be so cold. Surely Draco’s had frozen over and died in his chest by now.

He pulled her up from falling over the edge and they stood in silence as he finished his cigarette. Hermione could do nothing but watch the thick fog roll in across the grounds, the stars winking at her from above. It can be surprising how even the most incredible view can become distorted and dull in the face of misery, the young moon a blur of white through her glassy eyes.

The rest of the evening was cast in the bitter light of Draco’s control. She couldn’t even enjoy a night that had been his idea. A new year was meant for new beginnings, a fresh start—leaving the year behind and stepping into a new one but no matter how many years passed in Hermione’s sad little life they never changed, never became anything more than what they were, like the last ten years had been muddled into one very long harrowing one.

Not only did Draco get more drunk—the drinks never leaving his fist but he became aggressive towards her and anyone who looked in their direction. He was full of rude comments and dirty glares, creating a tense atmosphere that wasn’t fun for anyone.

“Maybe you should take him home,” Blaise managed to whisper in her ear. “He’s behaving like a complete nut job and scaring the guests.”

Hermione screwed her eyes shut with embarrassment, defeat sitting firmly on her shoulders. Draco was hot, heavy and so completely drunk as he leaned against her, playing with the diamond on her finger and smothering her with his constant touch.

“How do you suppose I do that? Look at him Blaise.”

Blaise tore his apprehensive eyes away from hers to his friend who couldn’t even stand straight, his eyes glassy and unfocused as he curled around her body like a dragon protecting a gem. Blaise swallowed hard, his brows pointing downwards with a lost hope that Draco would ever be anything other than who he was—a broken drunk.

“I'm sorry to do this to you Hermione but you’re gonna need to bribe him with something. He’s got
to go.”

Hermione nodded, her curls bouncing against her shoulders. Her stomach filled with rocks as she placed her hands on Draco's flushed cheeks, looking deep into his eyes and trying her best to get him to understand what she was saying.

“Draco, we need to leave. You’re very drunk and if you drink anymore you won’t be awake to see the surprise I have for you at home,” she smiled, her voice trained to talk to him gently like a child when he was in this unpredictable state.

His head straightened, his eyes blowing wide like that of a playful cat. “What surprise?” “Well if I told you it wouldn’t be a surprise now would it?”
He pulled her flush to his body, kissing her cheek and taking her hand. “Let’s go then.”

Hermione inhaled a deep breath, grateful for once that Draco couldn’t resist the pull of temptation. She discreetly nodded over to Blaise who still stood waiting in anticipation. She wanted so desperately to see Theo and apologise but she knew this was her one and only window to stop Draco from ruining the rest of the party for everyone else.

She pulled the silvery portkey from his blazer pocket and tried very poorly to drag Draco back through the hot crowd and out into the night. They walked back down the cobbled path between two manicured laws, her heels echoing against the stone, yellow firefly like lanterns guiding their way just as they had when they entered. As they reached the end of the property pops and whizzes filled the air, loud enough to damage an eardrum.

Burst of red, blue, green and gold filled the sky as the world lit up around them with bright sparkling fireworks, signaling the midnight hour of a new year. Hermione watched them as they exploded across the dark backdrop, leaving nothing but a fast moving cloud of smoke in their wake. She could hear the cheerful hoots and laughter of the party goers and their friends from inside the large estate, burning her eyes with tears that she couldn’t join them, that she couldn’t enjoy the beginning of a fresh year like a normal person.

Draco stood behind her, his hands at her hips as he watched, the multitude of colourful sparks reflecting in his eyes.

“Let’s go,” she forced herself to say.

Their fingers connected to the metal and Hermione grit her teeth as her stomach twisted and turned behind her navel with the pull of magical transportation. They landed heavily in a heap across the parlour floor, Draco's limp body covering hers. He crawled up onto all fours, looming over her.

“Where’s my surprise then?” He slurred.

Hermione’s heart thudded beneath her skin as she scrambled to come up with something believable but she wasn’t good at being put on the spot when it came to him.

“Erm..” she stumbled. “The surprise is, erm.. me.”

Draco's head jerked back in suspicion. “Baby, that’s not a surprise, that’s inevitable.”

He studied her closely, dragging his thumb across her bottom lip, his brows knit together. “What’s the real fucking reason you wanted to leave?” He accused.

Hermione closed her eyes to center herself, taking a deep breath. “You were behaving like an animal, Draco. You’re too drunk.” She didn’t want to mention that it wasn’t her idea and was in fact Blaise who asked for him to be removed.

He scoffed, dragging himself to his feet and pulling Hermione with him. “That’s the whole fucking idea of a party is it not? To get fucking balddered.”

“Not in the way you do it.”

He raised a brow. “And how do I do it?”

She crossed her arms over her chest, the beads of her dress weighing her down. “Undignified and quite frankly, embarrassing.”

She sucked in a sharp breath as he grabbed her upper arms, the cold moonlight shining in through the window and melting across his hard features, making him look more pointed than he already was.

“Embarrassing am I?” His lips tilted up into an amused smirk. “It’s just as well that I am because I need to fuck my wife. Take this dress off,” he demanded, sliding his hands around her ribs and pulling at the ribbons holding her corset together.

She pushed at his chest, knowing exactly where his actions would lead if she let them. Sex with Draco when he was drunk was never fun, it was always rough, hard and frenzied.

“Draco can I at least get some water first, my mouth is dry.” He breathed a long sigh, letting the ribbons drop. “Hurry up.”
Hermione didn’t let it show but her lungs sucked in a large relieved breath as she slipped from the parlour and into the Manor kitchens, sliding her feet from her heels as she went. The marble was cooling against her heels as she walked to the sink. She didn’t need water, she needed time. She needed a valid enough excuse to hopefully hold him off but she knew she had gotten herself into a mess. Something as simple as asking someone to leave a party because they were too drunk and intimidating was too much of an ask for Draco. She had to spin a lie and now the lie would come back to bite her.

She could see the large wall enclosing the rose garden from the window of the kitchen and wanted so badly to get lost amongst the flowers, even in this cold weather it wouldn’t have been as frosty as the chill of fear in her veins at that moment. She turned, her back against the sink as she looked around the empty space. It was so quiet in here, every sound echoed like she was in a tunnel, there was nothing for the sound to soak into, it was barren just like the rest of the house.

There wasn’t even a fruit bowl on the counter, the only thing that remained was a block with six sterling knives that Hermione had asked Draco to order for her since she wanted to cook the muggle way and cut everything by hand just for something slightly stimulating to do. Those knives were the only sign that the kitchen was even used. Everything here was self cleaning—courtesy of Malfoy Magic and everything was ordered from Diagon Alley to the Manor so shopping and cleaning were out. The only thing human she had left was to cook.

She circled the island trying to wrack her brain on what to say to Draco, feeling the thick anticipation build the longer she spent away from him. She had just reached the other side of the

kitchen where the knives were kept when he entered, filling the entire doorway with his broad shoulders as he swayed, fuming.

“Draco..I—“

She never got the chance to finish her sentence, he grabbed a fistful of her hair and spun her around, bending her harshly over the counter, the edge digging into her abdomen. Her cheek met the cold surface as did her hands as he ripped through the expensive fabric of her dress, the scatter of diamonds could be heard as they rolled across the floor in all directions. She felt the stale Manor air lick against her naked skin as the rest of her clothing was torn away.

“Draco please,” she cried, trying to push herself backwards, the movement only spurred him on.

She heard his zip and felt the hot tip of his cock breach her entrance, plunging into her with so much force that the marble cut into her hip bones, forcing a shriek of discomfort from her throat.

He pulled her head backwards, bending her neck with his grip in her curls. “I told you I was going to wreck you and I intend to keep to my word, sweetheart,” he growled coldly.

Hermione’s whole body erupted with fire, both from pain and anger. She curled her firsts, gritting her teeth. She didn’t know what possessed her to take it as far as she did that day but Hermione had enough. She had enough of being treated like his sex doll, his tiny little wife he could throw around whenever he pleased. She was tired of being a fucking prisoner.

Everything happened fast and in slow motion all at once as she ripped a knife from the block and threw her arm back, plunging the blade into his abdomen. Hermione heard his gasp of shock and then his pained wail as he pulled out of her and stumbled backwards against the kitchen island. The knife in her hand clattered to the ground, spinning in a pool of blood as it dripped from the silver.
Draco slapped a hand to his bleeding wound as a plethora of emotions flashed across his face.

Shock, pain, anger, disbelief.. hurt.

Chapter 12: Restitution in Ashes.



I will return to you.
I will lay down my weapons on this hill of memories and wait for you. If it takes so long that I dissolve into dust then stand over where my body laid, search for pieces of me in the sky and feel the earth shake as I rise.
For no grave can hold me from coming back to you.



She watched as his face paled and the white of his suit soaked with crimson. It flowed between his fingers, running from the edge of the cotton and spattering against his perfectly shined shoes. She watched his throat bob hard with a pained swallow as he slid down the island and collapsed onto the floor. Hermione was frozen to where she stood above him, her mouth agape in pure disturbance at what she had just done.

She pieced her ripped dress back together and kneeled beside him. Her hands were shaking with the hollow sound of his struggled breaths. Her mouth continued to hang open as she looked around her, she felt dizzy, like she wasn’t in control of her own body and this wasn’t happening. Her heart was pounding so hard that she could feel it behind her eyes. In her panic she almost attempted to cast vulnera sanentur but Draco's voice cut through the haze.

“Hermione,” he breathed, his hand searching for hers, his wedding band winked at her in the pale moonlight, painting his skin a cold shade of blue. She took it nervously, feeling the hot wet of his blood sticking their palms together and suddenly the monster inside him fell silent with her warmth.

His touch pulled her out of the shocked state she had fallen into, heaving sobs breaking from her chest like waves as her eyes flooded with tears.

“I’m sorry,” she whimpered, barely able to get the words past her trembling throat. She felt like she couldn’t breathe. “I wish things were different.”

He looked at her, his eyes were bloodshot and veiled in pain but they held a softness, like he knew he was dying and it tore Hermione’s heart to shreds.

“I don’t,” he choked, dark red blood sputtering through the lines in his pale lips, his words caught in a wheeze of agony. “I had ten years with the only woman I ever loved.”

Her heart cinched sharply. “You never loved me, Draco.”

His hand tried to tighten around hers like it always did in the height of an emotion but he was growing too weak. His blood pooled around him, soaking into her from where she sat beside him.

His face twisted with sadness. “I did Hermione, I do. Just never in the way you deserved.”

She closed her eyes, streams of tears coating her cheeks as she tried to cling onto what life he had left. “I wish we got to have a happily ever after,” she whispered brokenly.

He coughed, more blood spraying out across his handsome face, sweat dotting his forehead, dampening his blonde fringe.

“I never.. cared about a happy.. ending, Hermione. I just.. wanted you.”

He was struggling to speak now, his chest rising and falling rapidly between rattling gasps. ”I’ll wait.. for you.”

Those fearful eyes beneath his hooded lids slit her soul up like a razor and the sound of his dying voice would echo within her forever. Hermione said nothing more as she placed her cheek against his ribs and lay with him in his growing pool of ichor until she heard the last thump of her husband’s heart, his hand falling limp from hers. Her tears soaked through his shirt, mixing with his blood as she cried against him, fisting the white fabric like it would somehow bring him back to her.

She was now completely and utterly alone, the quiet darkness had never felt so terrifying. Draco would never be there to comfort her again. She would never get to witness the glow of his blue eyes or his white smile, would she forget about the freckle under his eye and the way it moved with his laughter?

When everything is bad, only the good comes back to haunt you.

His last words spoken were so pure that a part of her deeply regretted her choices. She trembled violently, her tears dripping down his cheeks as she leant over and placed one last soft kiss against his cold growing lips. She could still taste the whisky and smell the sweet leather of him that she had always loved. Her heart squeezed with the ghost of an iron fist as she dragged herself away from his dead body, washed in all that she had left of him.

Hermione’s legs carried her zombie-like through the desolate hallways but her heart still remained with him there.. on that cold kitchen floor.

She was full of conflicting emotions as she always was. She pulled her unused and dusty wand from a draw as she went, stepping out onto the grounds and collapsing onto the stone, her knees splitting open as grief ripped through every inch of her. Her eyes burning and her cheeks stinging with punishment, with her own self inflicted pain.

Hermione looked up at the dark looming Manor above her and spat at it. This house, these bricks, these damn memories had destroyed everything. She never would have thought when she was at Hogwarts that ten years later she would be sitting in front of Malfoy Manor with her murdered husband inside, she never would have thought that after ten painful years she would have absolutely nothing to show for it but scars.

Her lips curled in fury as she stood, bloodied and broken. She raised her wand and set the cursed house alight with all the fiendfyre she could muster. High angry, orange flames danced in her red rimmed eyes as it ripped through the Manor and swallowed the body belonging to the love of her life. Memories, both good and bad burning alongside him as the dark sky lit up with the flickering shades of amber, casting eerie shadows across the ground.

The barely beating organ in her chest threatened to rip itself in half with the heavy weight of her sorrow. Sorrow that the beautiful man she had looked at everyday for the last ten years couldn’t have been the one. Couldn’t have loved her right, in the way she wanted and so desperately needed. All she ever wanted was to be loved. But instead he had mangled her so badly that the only way to escape her hell was to set it on fire.

She had lived in hell for so long that she had mistaken it for a home and called it love.

The part of her heart that ached longed to burn with him, to sit amongst the flames and ignite with his empty shell but her head would not allow her to be so foolish. She had risked everything to get here and she would not burn.

She clenched her teeth at the smell of burning flesh that reached her through the smoke, forcing her to breathe in the reality of her actions. The edges of her dress were singed, her skin coated in a layer of dark ash and the heat from the fire licked against her body, her curls blowing back from her face with the ferocity. There was no denying how maniacal she looked, bare foot in a golden gown and surrounded by the blazing remains of the infamous Malfoy Manor, the place she was imprisoned and forced to live.

The old building seemed to hiss as it caved in against the smothering fires. Every tear soaked, blood stained piece of fabric disintegrating down into the earth with its Lord.

She hated him just as much as she loved him for all that he had taken from her and for all that she would never get back. It had always been the case and the two feelings had never been separate, there was never any fine line between them. They were one and the same.

But revenge is survival and revenge is getting out.

Revenge is finally breaking the fucking cycle because you cannot kill what is already dead.

Hermione watched across the distance as the wards around the Manor cracked like a thin sheet of ice and fell. She saw her freedom paved into the stone road at the end of the driveway and ran for it. Her breath stuttered in her chest the closer she got to breaking free from her chains, stepping eagerly over the boundary line.. but nothing was ever as good as it seemed and that was too good to be true.

Every inch of Hermione’s skin opened in deep lacerations, blood spilling from her far too quickly and leaving her standing in a growing puddle of it, she gasped with the fiery pain of knives she couldn’t see until her feet stumbled back over onto Malfoy soil and the wounds knit themselves back together immediately.

Whatever fucked up spell Draco had used really did tie her to the Manor forever. It was stay.. or leave and die.
“You fucking bastard!”

The sound of her distressed scream echoed around the charred bits of Draco's home and far out across the acres of land into nothing, reaching no one but large black ravens that flew from the trees and down the road infront of her as if taunting her with the freedom she could taste but would never get.

“I fucking hate you,” she cried, her knees buckling and once again taking a blow on the sharp gravel but Hermione couldn’t feel it, she couldn’t feel anything as the world closed in on her.






Hermione slept under the gazebo in the rose garden that night in nothing more than her tattered, smoke scented dress. She cast a warming charm, thankful she still had her wand even though she could do wandless magic but having her vinewood was like being draped in a small comfort blanket.

The world was so incredibly, painfully quiet. She could see her freedom through the iron bars of the gates but she would never reach it. The fire had died and all that was left were ashes that whipped around in the breeze. She couldn’t stop thinking about Draco, about his face, about the sound of his voice, those were just a few things about him that would only ever exist now in her memory.

Hermione couldn’t wrap her head around the act of death, how someone could be there one moment and gone the next and no matter how many shadows of the earth and corners of the sky you searched, you would never find them again. All that was left was the debilitating hurt from the absence of someone who was once there, the grief of wanting to go to the only home you had ever known in a person but not being able to because he was gone.

Hermione couldn’t help but admit that when she plunged that knife into Draco’s skin, a part of her had died along with him. She loved him until his last breath and beyond.

Grief is what we get for loving too deeply.

Daylight came and went and she walked the frozen grounds aimlessly, stuck in her own pained loop and haunted by memories. Not only was she physically isolated but she had made a choice—an irrational and irreversible one that she wasn’t even sure was the right one anymore. But the most crippling thing about that was the fact that there was nothing she could do, she had made the decision to end his life to save her own but he had already condemned her. Hermione foolishly thought that with his death would bring her freedom and the spell would break.. but no.

She had not seen Narcissa like she expected, she briefly wondered if she was angry with her for killing her son or angry with her for sending him over to her.

The more time that passed after his death the louder the voice in her head got until she recognised it as his, whispering to her beyond the veil.

“Remember my vows, love?” His hollow words broke through her soul, devastating her further as he continued. “You’re stuck with me in life and death, Hermione. You cannot kill what was never alive.”

Her body was wracked with shivers, she threw her hands over her ears in hopes that it would drown him out and it did. But she could still feel him around her, she caught whiffs of his leathery scent on the wind, she saw his face staring back at her through the reflection of the pond she had tried and failed to drown in.

She had been dead but not allowed to die, alive but as good as dead.

Even in his death he couldn’t leave her alone and he had warned her of this from the very beginning. No matter if he was alive or dead he would make sure that she would always be his. She would never leave, she would never live, she would never love again.

She wandered around the property for two days with only the spell aguamenti to keep her body alive, until Lucius showed up on his old and burnt to the ground doorstep. He found her on his wife’s bench, sitting with her arms around her knees, her dress falling apart around her. Hermione didn’t even look at him as he sat beside her, she kept her dead eyes fixed ahead on a decaying rose head.

“Where is Draco?”

Hermione’s bottom lip quivered as she croaked out the words. “Dead.”

She heard him slowly suck in a lung full of air before he released it, not giving away any shock or sharp inhales, only cool, calm and collected breaths but she didn’t miss the shiny glint of a tear that he quickly wiped from his cheek.

“Can you get me out of here, Lucius?” She asked, her voice weak with dying desperation, watching as wisps of heat escaped her lips, out into the icy air.

He shook his blonde head slowly, his long hair brushing against her arm. “I cannot. I’m afraid no other magic in the world can break a soul that has been tied to the Malfoy grounds. It’s magic is too ancient and too powerful, it is that way for a reason.”

Hermione whipped her head in his direction, hugging her knees to her chest harder. “So you can keep all your women imprisoned?” She snapped, her lip curling up in disgust.

She saw his hand tighten across the snake head of his cane before he spoke. “What will you do?”

She looked away, tearing her eyes from his in hopes that it would sting. “I don’t know. What can I do, he’s forbidden me from fucking leaving even though there is no Manor and no Draco anymore.”

Saying the words out loud hurt far more than she expected it to. She had thought over and over about the situation but she had yet to speak the words out into the world and now she had, she had even less hope than before. Two days was the longest she had ever been without seeing Draco in nine years, she was always accustomed to his presence and even though she wanted to, she couldn’t be comfortable in her own company. Being with someone for any length of time will form an attachment, but ten years with someone as smothering as Draco built a dependency that she couldn’t break.

It was cruel, the way her mind tortured her with thoughts of him. She tried to tell herself that he was evil, reminding herself that he was a rapist, a murderer but she still fucking loved him. She just wanted it to go away, she just wanted her bleeding, love sick, weak heart to stop aching for him.
She had hoped that the anger would be enough and it was in moments but it never lasted beyond that.

And now she was a childless grieving widow with no where to go but back to him.

Everyone had left her because of him—Ron, Harry, her parents. It all hurt, it all brought her down to her knees with pain. Thinking of Theo and Blaise was a torment that wouldn’t go away either.. she had no way of getting in contact with them. She had tried every spell she knew to get past Draco's magic but nothing worked and she was too dead inside to try and send them a patronus.
Every which way she turned there was an obstacle, she was still just as trapped but now she was trapped, lonely and homeless.

Lucius left at some point—when he decided to leave, she had no clue. She was unfocused and uncaring. He couldn’t do anything to help her anyway. She wondered if her brain imagined him ever even being there.

Hermione had fallen into a perpetual state of numbness with only Draco’s sporadic whispers for company. She could feel him edging her towards something. The pull back to the Manor where his

remains lie was strong, she could still feel the faint burn from her ring if she walked too far away from it.

On the morning of the third day Hermione heaved with sickness but she had nothing in her stomach to expel. She was starving, tired, cold, lifeless and no one knew how long she had been living with a scream inside her—her scream, that no one else could hear no matter how loud and earth shattering it got.

Draco appeared to her in many different ways—whispers, the ghost of a soft fingertip tracing the length of her spine, a light tug of her hair. Hermione sat next to the pond and stared blankly at the ruins she had created. Her mind had been scooped out of any other thought and filled with only images of him.

“Join me, my love.”

She was so deeply tortured, every waking moment was like being ripped apart until she decided that today would be the day, the decision had been an easy one, one that was always destined for her. She couldn’t take it anymore, Draco would get his wish and with that thought came a sick kind of relief, the weight lifted from her shoulders as she stood and walked towards the leftover brick that once made a grand Manor.

Hermione lifted her wand, relighting the fire. She watched the dancing flames as they engulfed what was left and she swore she could see Draco’s silhouette through the silver smoke on the other side, waiting for her beyond the veil just as he said he would. She watched through watery eyes as the shadow raised his hand and she couldn’t hesitate any longer. Hermione stopped breathing as she stepped into the blaze, feeling the edges of her dress catch fire and burn around her. Her skin beaded with perspiration, the heat swarming her and drawing her closer to Draco’s ghost.

