The grounds were eerily quiet, the pond too still. The sky above him was dark with rolling thunderclouds and the musky scent of death was heavy in the air. The concrete angels at the entrance were weeping and this place would never see the rays of sunlight again.

He sat in the dirt, amongst the blackened shards of what once was a place he visited often as a child. But those memories weren’t always good ones.

He knew how tortured Draco was, how bad his home life was, he never spoke about it but Theo always knew. He had seen the bruises that Draco tried to hide, the emotions across his face that he tried to hold back, even as a kid. And then Draco fell even further into the deep dark pit of despair with his mother’s suicide and untimely death.

When he got into a relationship with Hermione it was a cycle that Theo could immediately see repeating, he treated her in the same learned fashion that Lucius had treated Narcissa. Draco had watched it all for years and was shaped by the wrong hands but it was never any fault of his own. When you’ve grown up accustomed to the certain way of things it’s only natural to carry them with you through life no matter how sinister they may be and if you didn’t know the pain that came with that, you were the lucky ones.

Theo did what he could to stop it but Hermione had begged him not to get involved, her sweet soul didn’t want him getting hurt and he had always been scared of Draco and his temper.

He and his precious Hermione had shared a special friendship, it was a friendship he had never had with another woman before and it was a friendship he would never find again.

They were both gone now with not even a body to bury, no grave to visit, no roses to lay. Their ashes would be forever lost between the rubble of what once was.

Tears dripped from the edge of his chin, soaking into the foundation as he layed his palm flat against the ground, trying to feel what was left of them through the stone.

“You have no idea how much I’ll miss you both. I hope you get the rest you need now, you both deserve peace. Your hearts may have become ashes but your souls will burn forever.”

Theo left through the iron gates, letting them slam shut behind him, the sharp creak shattering his heart as he walked down the road and never looked back.


